---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DraftNotice from "../components/DraftNotice.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About this insurance education and comparison project."
>
  <DraftNotice
    note="This About page is a draft and has not yet been reviewed by an insurance-regulatory lawyer. Wording may change before this site is used publicly."
  />
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About this site</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      This site helps people understand and compare U.S. car and home
      insurance. It is educational — it is not an insurance company, not a
      licensed insurance agency or broker, and nothing here is a quote or an
      offer to sell insurance.
    </p>

    <h2>What we do</h2>
    <p>
      We research car and home insurance rules state by state, tracing each
      one back to a regulator, a government body, or an insurer's own
      published terms. A rule is only published on this site once it's been
      verified that way — see the <a href="/auto-insurance/">auto</a> and
      <a href="/home-insurance/">home</a> pages for what's been verified so
      far. We also publish plain-language guides on how insurance pricing and
      coverage generally work (see <a href="/learn/">Learn</a>), and tools
      like the <a href="/tools/">Coverage Gap Checker</a> that compare the
      numbers you enter against those verified rules.
    </p>

    <h2>What we don't do, for now</h2>
    <p>
      This site does not collect leads, does not create accounts, does not
      ask you to upload documents, and does not provide actual insurance
      quotes. Nothing you type into a tool here is saved or sent anywhere —
      see our <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> page. Any future referral,
      lead-generation, or advertising features would only launch after legal
      review, and would be clearly disclosed — see
      <a href="/how-we-make-money/">How We Make Money</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How we decide what to publish</h2>
    <p>
      Every fact we publish carries a source link and a "last verified" date.
      Facts that come from a secondary source, that conflict between sources,
      or that haven't been researched yet are kept out of public pages —
      they're tracked internally as research to-do items, not shown as fact.
      If a state or topic isn't covered yet, the page says "Coming soon"
      instead of guessing.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Not an insurance company, agent, or broker</li>
      <li>Not a source of legal, financial, or insurance advice</li>
      <li>Not a quote — any numbers shown are educational estimates or reference data, clearly labeled as such</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Always confirm anything important with your insurer or a licensed
      insurance professional before making a coverage decision.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
